    "q-95640": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95640",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "$$\n\n$\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "The derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
